TITLE: Task 6 - Critical Evaluation of Experimental Design for Hospital Readmission

CONTENT:

=============================================================================
TASK 6: CRITICAL EVALUATION OF EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN
=============================================================================

CONTEXT:
The hospital readmission analysis presented in this consultancy is based on observational
data (retrospective analysis of historical admissions). Task 6 asks: How could experimental
design methodologies improve future investigations of hospital readmission?

Two experimental designs are evaluated: Completely Randomized Design (CRD) and Randomized
Complete Block Design (RCBD).

=============================================================================
1. COMPLETELY RANDOMIZED DESIGN (CRD) APPLICABILITY
=============================================================================

DEFINITION:
CRD involves randomly assigning patients to treatment or control groups, ensuring
confounding variables are balanced through randomization alone.

EXAMPLE APPLICATION FOR READMISSION:

Design: "Efficacy of 48-Hour Post-Discharge Follow-Up on Readmission Rates"
- Population: 300 diabetic patients discharged from hospital
- Randomization: 
  * Intervention group (n=150): Receive nurse-led phone call 48 hours post-discharge
  * Control group (n=150): Receive standard discharge instructions only
- Primary outcome: Readmission within 30 days
- Duration: 6-month recruitment + 30-day follow-up = 8 months total

ADVANTAGES OF CRD FOR READMISSION STUDIES:

1. Causal Inference
   ✓ CRD eliminates confounding through randomization
   ✓ Can claim: "Follow-up calls CAUSE lower readmission" (not just associated)
   ✓ Stronger evidence than observational study

2. Simplicity
   ✓ Straightforward randomization process
   ✓ Simple statistical analysis (t-tests, chi-square)
   ✓ Easy to explain to hospital administration

3. Efficiency
   ✓ Fixed sample size calculation (80% power = ~590 patients total)
   ✓ Clear endpoint (30-day readmission)
   ✓ Relatively quick timeline (8 months feasible)

4. Clinical Relevance
   ✓ Tests specific, actionable intervention
   ✓ Results directly translate to hospital protocol changes
   ✓ Can estimate Number-Needed-to-Treat (NNT)

DISADVANTAGES OF CRD FOR READMISSION STUDIES:

1. Control Heterogeneity
   ✗ Cannot control for hospital-level factors (all patients from 1 hospital)
   ✗ Staff differences, facility differences unaccounted for
   ✗ Results may not generalize to other hospitals

2. Risk Stratification
   ✗ Cannot test if intervention works differently for high-risk vs. low-risk patients
   ✗ One-size-fits-all approach may miss subgroup effects
   ✗ May waste resources on low-risk patients who don't need intensive follow-up

3. Ethical Considerations
   ✗ May be unethical to withhold follow-up from control group
   ✗ Solution: Compare intensities (standard vs. enhanced follow-up) not (treatment vs. nothing)

4. Feasibility
   ✗ Requires sustained patient contact (loss to follow-up common)
   ✗ Clinic staff burden (phone calls, documentation)
   ✗ Blinding impossible (know if getting phone call)

FEASIBILITY ASSESSMENT FOR HOSPITAL READMISSION:
MODERATE TO HIGH FEASIBILITY
- CRD is viable for single-hospital intervention pilot
- Estimated cost: $50,000-75,000
- Estimated timeline: 8-10 months
- Likelihood of implementation: 60-70%

=============================================================================
2. RANDOMIZED COMPLETE BLOCK DESIGN (RCBD) APPLICABILITY
=============================================================================

DEFINITION:
RCBD involves dividing patients into homogeneous blocks BEFORE randomization, then
randomly assigning intervention within each block. This reduces within-block variability
and allows stratified analysis.

EXAMPLE APPLICATION FOR READMISSION:

Design: "Risk-Stratified Intervention Study for Readmission Prevention"

Blocking Strategy (by predicted readmission risk):
- Block 1 (Low risk, <8%): 100 patients
  * 50 → Standard discharge
  * 50 → Enhanced discharge (control for this block)
  
- Block 2 (Moderate risk, 8-15%): 100 patients
  * 50 → Intensive phone follow-up (2 calls)
  * 50 → Standard phone follow-up (1 call)
  
- Block 3 (High risk, >15%): 100 patients
  * 50 → Intensive case management (multiple interventions)
  * 50 → Standard intensive intervention (control for this block)

Total: 300 patients randomized within risk strata

PRIMARY OUTCOME: Readmission within 30 days (by block and overall)

ADVANTAGES OF RCBD FOR READMISSION STUDIES:

1. Risk-Stratified Analysis
   ✓ Answers: Does intervention work equally for all risk levels?
   ✓ Can identify: Which risk groups benefit most?
   ✓ Enables personalized medicine (tailor intervention to risk)

2. Statistical Efficiency
   ✓ Blocks reduce unexplained variance (blocks vs. unblocked)
   ✓ Same sample size yields more statistical power
   ✓ Can detect smaller treatment effects

3. Subgroup Analysis
