CONTENT:

=============================================================================
TASK 8: EVALUATION OF BAYESIAN STATISTICAL METHODS
=============================================================================

Three Bayesian approaches evaluated for hospital readmission prediction:
1. Naïve Bayes Classifier
2. Bayesian Regression
3. Bayesian Decision Making

=============================================================================
1. NAÏVE BAYES CLASSIFIER
=============================================================================

DEFINITION:
Naïve Bayes applies Bayes' theorem assuming all features are conditionally independent
given the outcome class. Simple, interpretable probabilistic classifier.

FORMULA:
P(Readmitted | Features) = P(Features | Readmitted) × P(Readmitted) / P(Features)

Naive assumption: P(Age, Meds, LOS | Readmitted) = P(Age|Readmitted) × P(Meds|Readmitted) × P(LOS|Readmitted)

APPLICABILITY TO READMISSION:

Advantages:
✓ Fast training (computationally cheap)
✓ Works with limited data
✓ Interpretable (probability distributions of each feature)
✓ Baseline classifier for comparison

Disadvantages:
✗ Independence assumption violated (age & comorbidities correlated)
✗ Poor discrimination compared to logistic regression
✗ Ignores feature interactions (e.g., age × polypharmacy)

WHEN TO USE:
- Quick baseline model
- Limited training data (<1000 samples)
- Interpretability critical, accuracy secondary
- Mobile/edge deployment (minimal computational resources)

RECOMMENDATION FOR READMISSION: 
Use as BASELINE ONLY. Logistic regression (Task 5) superior.

---

=============================================================================
2. BAYESIAN REGRESSION
=============================================================================

DEFINITION:
Bayesian logistic regression assigns probability distributions to coefficients rather
than point estimates. Outputs posterior distributions reflecting uncertainty.

ADVANTAGES FOR READMISSION:

✓ Incorporate Prior Knowledge
  Example: Medical literature shows age increases readmission risk
  Prior: β_age ~ Normal(mean=0.05, sd=0.02)  [positive effect]
  Posterior combines prior + observed data
  Result: More stable estimates, less overfitting

✓ Uncertainty Quantification
  Frequentist: β_age = 0.045 [95% CI: 0.040-0.050]
  Bayesian: β_age ~ Normal(0.047, 0.003)
  Direct probability: P(β_age > 0 | data) = 99.2%
  More intuitive for decision-making

✓ Small Sample Properties
  Bayesian estimates more stable when n small
  Prior information stabilizes estimates

✓ Hierarchical Modeling
  Multiple hospitals with shared + hospital-specific effects
  Better than separate models per hospital

✓ Probabilistic Predictions
  Natural output: P(Readmitted | Patient) = 0.65 (not just 0 or 1)
  Useful for risk stratification thresholds

DISADVANTAGES FOR READMISSION:

✗ Prior Specification Burden
  Must specify distributions for each coefficient
  Subjective; requires domain expertise
  Sensitivity: Results depend on prior choice

✗ Computational Complexity
  MCMC sampling required (slower than MLE)
  Convergence diagnostics needed
  Requires statistical sophistication

✗ Implementation Difficulty
  Not standard in clinical software
  Requires specialized packages (Stan, PyMC3)
  Harder to deploy in hospital EHR

✗ Interpretability Trade-off
  Posterior distributions less intuitive than coefficients
  Clinicians prefer simple risk scores

WHEN TO USE BAYESIAN REGRESSION:

✓ Multi-hospital system (hierarchical model)
✓ Strong prior knowledge from literature
✓ Need uncertainty quantification for high-stakes decisions
✓ Small sample size with prior information available
✓ Research setting vs. clinical deployment

BAYESIAN DECISION MAKING APPLICATION:

Hospital's cost structure:
- False negative (miss high-risk): $20,000 (readmission cost + penalty)
- False positive (flag non-readmit): $500 (unnecessary intervention)

Bayesian decision theory finds optimal threshold:
- Instead of threshold = 0.5
- Optimal threshold = 0.4 (lower threshold, catch more readmissions)
- Minimizes expected cost

Expected utility = P(readmit|high) × (-20000) + P(no readmit|high) × (-500)

This natural framework for clinical decision-making.

RECOMMENDATION FOR READMISSION:

IMPLEMENT BAYESIAN LOGISTIC REGRESSION WITH:

1. Informative Priors from Literature
   β_age ~ Normal(0.03, 0.01)           [age increases readmission]
   β_medications ~ Normal(0.05, 0.02)   [polypharmacy increases readmission]
   β_prior_visits ~ Normal(0.04, 0.01)  [high utilization increases risk]
   Other features: weakly informative priors

2. Posterior Inference
   Use MCMC sampling to generate posterior distributions
   Report: mean, 95% credible intervals, tail probabilities

3. Decision Framework
   Compute optimal classification threshold based on hospital's cost function
   Recommend: Threshold = 0.40 (prioritize sensitivity)

4. Deployment
   Risk dashboard shows:
   - Point estimate: P(Readmitted) = 0.65
   - Uncertainty: 95% credible interval [0.58-0.72]
   - Actionability: High probability AND narrow interval → confident prediction

ADVANTAGES OVER FREQUENTIST:
- Directly answers: P(β_age > 0 | data) = 99%
  Frequentist can only say: "If true effect is 0, we'd see this data 2% of the time"
- Incorporates external knowledge
- Natural probabilistic framework for clinical decisions
- Handles multiple comparisons naturally (no multiple testing correction)

=============================================================================
3. COMPARISON: FREQUENTIST VS. BAYESIAN
=============================================================================

ASPECT                    | FREQUENTIST              | BAYESIAN
--------------------------|--------------------------|---------------------------
Parameter Estimate        | Point + CI               | Posterior distribution
Uncertainty               | 95% CI (hard to interpret)| Credible interval (intuitive)
Prior Info                | Ignored                  | Incorporated
"Probability of Effect"   | Not available            | Direct: P(β > 0 | data)
Multiple Comparisons      | Bonferroni correction    | Naturally handled
Sample Size Determination | Fixed (power analysis)   | Can be adaptive
Interpretation            | Frequentist (repeats)    | Bayesian (this data)
Hospital Cost Integration | Not natural              | Natural utility framework

FOR HOSPITAL READMISSION:
Bayesian approach better aligns with clinical decision-making

=============================================================================
4. IMPLEMENTATION FOR HOSPITAL READMISSION
=============================================================================

RECOMMENDED HYBRID APPROACH:

1. Primary Model: Logistic Regression (Task 5)
   - Deployed in EHR
   - Simple risk score (0-100%)
   - Real-time calculations

2. Secondary Model: Bayesian Logistic Regression
   - Research/validation tool
   - Quantifies uncertainty
   - Optimizes decision thresholds
   - Incorporates clinical knowledge

3. Decision Framework: Bayesian
   - Cost matrix: false negative vs. false positive
   - Optimal threshold: ~0.40 (not 0.50)
   - Risk stratification: Low/Moderate/High
   - Follow-up intensity matched to uncertainty

4. Dashboard Integration:
   Display both:
   ├─ Point Risk Estimate: 65%
   ├─ 95% Credible Interval: [58%-72%]
   ├─ Recommended Action: Intensive follow-up
   └─ Confidence Level: High (narrow interval)

=============================================================================
5. CONCLUSION
=============================================================================

Naïve Bayes: Simple baseline; not recommended for deployment.

Bayesian Regression: RECOMMENDED for research and decision support.
- Aligns with clinical reasoning
- Quantifies uncertainty
- Incorporates expert knowledge
- Natural framework for cost-benefit analysis

Implement as TWO-TIER SYSTEM:
- Tier 1: Logistic regression in EHR (real-time risk score)
- Tier 2: Bayesian analysis for validation, threshold optimization, and uncertainty

This hybrid approach combines ease-of-use (logistic) with sophistication (Bayesian).

