"We built three predictive models to identify high-risk patients at discharge.

[CLICK to show models]

First: Logistic Regression. This is a classic statistical approach that calculates
a probability between 0 and 100% that a patient will readmit within 30 days.

Second: Ridge Regression. This handles the problem where our predictor variables
are correlated (like age and comorbidities often go together). Ridge regression
'shrinks' the coefficients to prevent overfitting.

Third: LASSO Regression. This automatically selects the most important features
while discarding less important ones—useful for a simpler, more interpretable model.

[CLICK to show comparison table]

We evaluated all three on a test set of 30,000 patients. Here are the results:

Logistic Regression came out on top with 75% AUC—that means if we pick a random
readmitted patient and a random non-readmitted patient, our model correctly ranks
the readmitted patient as higher-risk 75% of the time.

Sensitivity is 71%—meaning we catch 71% of patients who will actually readmit.
Specificity is 73%—meaning we correctly identify 73% of patients who won't readmit.

For clinical implementation, we chose Logistic Regression because it's fully
interpretable—we can explain to clinicians exactly what drives each patient's risk.

[CLICK to show top features]

These are the top 10 most important features: Age, number of medications, prior
emergency visits, length of stay, and discharge disposition are the strongest predictors.

This makes clinical sense. An elderly patient on many medications with frequent ED visits
and complex discharge is a textbook high-risk readmission case."

