Create file: 07_PRESENTATIONS/VIVA_PREP/Anticipated_Questions_and_Answers.docx

Likely Viva Questions:

Q1: "Why did you choose logistic regression over machine learning (e.g., random forests)?"
A: "Logistic regression offers better interpretability for clinical implementation. Hospital
staff need to understand why a patient is flagged as high-risk. Logistic coefficients directly
show each feature's impact. Random forests are 'black boxes'—we can't explain predictions
to clinicians. For readmission prevention, interpretability is critical."

Q2: "How did you handle class imbalance (11% readmitted, 89% not)?"
A: "We used stratified train/test split to ensure class distribution consistent in both sets.
We report sensitivity and specificity separately (not just accuracy). AUC-ROC is invariant to
class imbalance—more appropriate metric than accuracy. We would also recommend adjusting the
classification threshold to prioritize sensitivity (catch readmissions) over specificity."

Q3: "What are the limitations of your model?"
A: "Limitations: (1) Observational data—we can show associations not causation. (2) Single
hospital data—may not generalize. (3) Model assumes patients, medications, and outcomes
constant (in reality, things change). (4) Doesn't capture unmeasured confounders (e.g., social
factors). (5) Readmission can be unplanned or potentially preventable; we don't distinguish.
We recommend validation on hospital's data before deployment."

Q4: "How did expert feedback influence your recommendations?"
A: "[Expert name] emphasized that resource constraints are the real bottleneck. Many hospitals
CAN identify high-risk patients but DON'T have capacity for intensive follow-up. This shaped
our HREWS recommendation to include tiered protocols—intensive follow-up only for highest-risk.
Also confirmed that medication reconciliation is THE critical intervention."

Q5: "Why pursue an RCT? Isn't observational data sufficient?"
A: "Observational data shows associations. RCT shows causation. Currently we can say 'high-risk
patients who receive intensive follow-up have lower readmission rates'—but maybe they readmit
less because they're healthier to begin with. RCT randomly assigns intensive follow-up, proving
it CAUSES readmission reduction. Necessary for strong evidence to justify hospital-wide adoption."

Q6: "What would you do differently if starting over?"
A: "With hindsight: (1) Include qualitative data (patient interviews about discharge experience)
not just quantitative. (2) Collect clinical trajectory data (daily vitals) to predict discharge
safety. (3) Engage patients earlier as stakeholders. (4) Start with smaller pilot before full
consultancy. (5) Build in time for iterative feedback from clinical staff."

Q7: "How does this compare to other readmission prediction research?"
A: "Compared to published literature, our 75% AUC is solid but not exceptional—some studies
report 80-85% with more complex models. However, we prioritized interpretability, which trade
offs some accuracy. Our focus on implementation and evidence-based interventions is stronger
than many academic papers. HREWS design incorporating risk-stratified protocols is novel—
most literature focuses on prediction alone."

Q8: "How would this scale to multiple hospitals?"
A: "Current model is hospital-specific (trained on one hospital's data). Scaling approach:
(1) Hospital 1 pilots HREWS and builds local model. (2) Hospital 2 and 3 validate model on
their data—likely need recalibration for local readmission rates. (3) Bayesian hierarchical
modeling could share information across hospitals while respecting local variation. (4) Standard
implementation toolkit (dashboards, protocols, training) would be reusable. (5) Central learning
network shares outcomes and best practices."

